producer_report.summary:
Added a read-only `athena score-jev [--cut <time>] [--json]` command. It builds the labeled set from introspections that a reviewer labeled and scores Jev's stored shadow answers against those labels. All three checks pass: fmt, clippy with warnings denied, and the full test suite. The command has not been run over the real `records/`, which is out of scope.

**Why a new record kind.** No record of a person's review existed. I added `athena.class-label/1` in `crates/athena-data/src/records.rs`. It holds:
- the introspection it labels;
- the reviewed class (a registry id, or none);
- who labeled it (`person`, or `stronger-model` with a person's spot-check);
- when;
- optionally whether the event repeats.

The newest label per introspection counts. Nothing writes these records yet; the command only reads them. The store reader gained a `class_labels` field so the new variant doesn't hit its catch-all `expect`.

**Labeled set and scoring** (`crates/athena-core/src/labeled.rs`, read-only; a test asserts it never opens a file for writing):
- **Labeled set:** an introspection is a row if it has a label and a usable stored Jev answer. The report counts what was left out: unreviewed, reviewed with no Jev answer, reviewed with a failed Jev call, and labels that name no recorded introspection. Each row keeps its labeler.
- **Score:** Brier score of Jev's class probabilities against the label, where "none" is Jev's `new` option. The two references are always-none and word overlap. Skill is the improvement over the better reference.
- **Other measures:** top-1 accuracy, false-match rate on "none" labels, and an is-repeat Brier score with always-false and base-rate references, where a label says whether the event repeats.
- **Split:** by time. `--cut` sets the cut; by default the earliest two thirds calibrate. Calibration sets the word-overlap threshold and the repeat base rate. Only the test part is scored.
- **Interval:** 95% normal interval, given only when n > (1.96·sd/mean)² (§10.4 item 1). Otherwise the report says "no bound was computed" and shows the raw Brier scores and the per-record table.
- **Verdict:**
  - Items 1 and 3 are computed as §10.4 describes.
  - Item 2 is only the check that each reviewed group lands in one Jev class with no class mixing groups. It does not look up R1–R4 or the §3.6 cases.
  - Items 4–7 are shown as not evaluated.
  - The overall verdict is "not met", "not established", or "computed items met". The last is explicitly not a promotion.
- **Too few cases:** with fewer than `MIN_TEST_CASES` (10) test rows, the report says "too few labeled cases" and carries no score and no verdict. The 10 is my estimate, not a power calculation.

**Tests.**
- Core: 11 unit tests in `labeled.rs`. They use FakeJev answers through the real `ask_jev` and cover:
  - enough cases (score, sample size, no-bound note, verdict);
  - an interval case;
  - too few cases;
  - exclusion counts for unreviewed, no-Jev-answer and Jev-error records;
  - newest label wins and the labeler is kept;
  - Brier arithmetic, the interval rule, the default split and the overlap reference;
  - the write-guard.
- CLI: `crates/athena-cli/tests/labeled.rs` has 5 tests. They use fixture records in a temp git repo and cover:
  - the help listing;
  - an empty store with `--json`;
  - a full run that checks the repository is unchanged (HEAD and `git status` clean);
  - too few cases;
  - a bad `--cut`.
- Also added a records round-trip test for the new record in `athena-data`.

**Docs.** Design doc §12 step 6, CHANGELOG and the README command table are updated. They say what was built and what was not.
